902 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 338 0 R
/E (A typical microcontroller contains a central processing unit \(CPU\), program memory, data memory, clock-generation circuitry, reset circuitry, GPIO ports and peripheral modules. The CPU fetches instructions from program memory, decodes them and executes operations. Program memory stores the firmware, while data memory stores variables, temporary values and intermediate results during execution.GPIO pins provide the connection between the internal digital logic and external circuits. A GPIO pin may be configured as an input to read a switch or logic signal, or as an output to drive a control input. Some pins may also support alternate peripheral functions. The designer must therefore check the pin multiplexing information before allocating the controller pins.The STM8 family uses an 8-bit CPU architecture. The processor width describes the size of the basic data path and arithmetic operations; it does not mean that the complete application is limited to eight signals. Multiple I/O pins and external devices can be used to interface with a larger system. In this project, the shift-register chain is one method of extending the number of monitored input signals.Program memory stores the instructions that define the cable-testing sequence. The firmware is expected to initialize the controller, configure its I/O pins, establish the state of the external interfaces and manage the user interaction. During a test, it can generate the required control sequence, acquire input bits and compare the received pattern with an expected mapping.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
