/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 343 0 R
/E (The TPS563200 is a synchronous step-down DC-DC converter manufactured by Texas Instruments. A buck converter converts a higher DC input voltage into a lower regulated DC output. The project power sheet uses this device in a stage intended to convert a nominal 12 V input into a nominal 5 V supply rail. A switching regulator is generally selected when improved c)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
955 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 343 0 R
/E (A buck converter contains a controlled switching element, an inductor, output capacitance and a feedback/control circuit. During the switching interval in which the high-side switch conducts, energy is transferred from the input source to the inductor and load. The inductor current increases and energy is stored in its magnetic field.During the other interval, the inductor current continues to flow through the synchronous low-side switch. The inductor releases stored energy to the load and output capacitor. The output capacitor smooths the voltage and supplies current during switching transitions. The repeated switching action produces a lower average output voltage.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
956 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 343 0 R
/E (The duty cycle is the fraction of each switching period for which the high-side switch is active. In an ideal continuous-conduction buck converter, the output-to-input voltage ratio is approximately equal to the duty cycle. Real circuits have losses and control limitations, so the actual output must be designed and measured using the regulator datasheet.A synchronous buck converter uses a controlled MOSFET in place of a conventional freewheeling diode for the low-side current path. This can reduce conduction losses because the MOSFET's on-resistance may be lower than the forward voltage drop of a diode under the relevant operating conditions. The internal control circuit )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
957 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 343 0 R
/E (The inductor stores energy during the switching cycle and transfers it to the load. Its inductance affects current ripple and transient response. The inductor must have a saturation-current rating greater than the maximum expected peak current and a suitable RMS-current rating.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
958 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 343 0 R
/E (The inductor's DC resistance contributes to conduction loss and heating. Its physical package and height must also fit the PCB and enclosure. The final inductance and part number must be selected using the TI datasheet design guidance rather than copied from an unrelated regulator circuit.)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
959 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 343 0 R
/E (4.2.6 Output capacitor and voltage ripple)
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
960 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 343 0 R
/E (The output capacitor reduces voltage ripple and supplies transient load current. Its effective capacitance, equivalent series resistance and voltage rating influence the output behaviour. Ceramic capacitors can lose effective capacitance under DC bias, so the manufacturer's bias curves may need to be considered.The required output capacitance depends on the converter's control architecture and stability requirements. The exact recommended values and capacitor types should be taken from the TPS563200 datasheet and reference design. After assembly, the output ripple should be measured with an appropriate oscilloscope probing technique.A feedback network allows the converter to compare a scaled version of the output voltage with an internal reference. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
