/E (The 74HC165 is an eight-bit parallel-load, serial-output shift register. It is a digital logic device that allows several parallel input signals to be captured and then read sequentially through a serial output. This reduces the number of microcontroller pins needed to monitor multiple digital signals.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
990 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 346 0 R
/E (The cable tester uses three 74HC165 devices in a cascaded arrangement. Each device provides eight parallel input positions, giving a total of 24 input positions when all channels are used. The actual connector-to-bit mapping must be recorded from the schematic. The device has eight parallel data inputs. In the parallel-load condition, the logic state at each input is captured into the internal storage stages. The captured data remains available for serial readout after the device is placed into shift operation. The parallel-load control on standard 74HC165 devices is active-low. This means the load operation is selected when the control signal is at logic low. The exact timing relationship between the load signal, clock and data output must be checked in the selected manufacturer's timing diagram.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
991 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 346 0 R
/E (After the parallel inputs are captured, clock pulses move the stored bits through the shift register. The serial output presents one bit at a time. The microcontroller reads the output at the required time relative to the clock edge, according to the device timing specification.The bit order depends on the device's internal shift direction and the way the cascade is connected. Firmware should not assume that the first bit received corresponds to connector pin one. The actual bit order should be derived from the schematic and confirmed during bench testing.The parallel-load signal selects whether the device captures the parallel inputs or shifts stored data. The clock input advances the shift register. The clock-enable input controls whether clocking is enabled. The serial data input allows data to be shifted into the device and supports cascading. Signal names used in the project may differ from datasheet pin names. For example, a schematic may use SR_LOAD, SR_CLK and SR_CE. The designer should document the active polarity and function of each net and compare it with the selected device's pinout and truth table.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
992 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 346 0 R
/E (Cascading connects the serial output of one shift register to the serial input of the next. The devices share the control signals so that they load and shift in a coordinated manner. The controller can then obtain the data from multiple devices using a serial connection.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
993 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 346 0 R
/E (With three eight-bit devices, the chain represents 24 parallel input states. The serial output of the final device in the chain is connected to the controller's serial input according to the selected cascade direction. The first and last device positions should be clearly labelled in the schematic and firmware documentation. A chain diagram is useful for understanding the data path, but the actual net connections must be taken from the KiCad schematic. The chain should be checked for correct serial-output-to-serial-input connections and for shared load, clock and enable controls.)
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
