/P 347 0 R
/E (Each parallel input should be at a valid logic low or logic high when the data is captured. An unconnected input can float and produce an unpredictable state. Pull-up or pull-down resistors may be required depending on the electrical arrangement of the cable interface. The resistor values should be chosen by considering input leakage, noise immunity, current consumption and the cable environment. If the input is connected to an external connector, additional protection may be needed to handle electrostatic discharge or accidental voltage application. Digital logic devices specify timing requirements such as setup time, hold time, clock pulse width, propagation delay and maximum clock frequency. Setup time describes how long an input must be stable before the relevant clock event. Hold time describes how long it must remain stable afterward. The microcontroller firmware should generate control signals that satisfy the shift-register timing requirements. The clock frequency should be within the recommended operating range. Adequate delay may be required between parallel loading, shifting and sampling.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
1003 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 347 0 R
/E (The shift registers must operate from a supply voltage compatible with the controller and input logic. The supply and ground connections must follow the exact datasheet. A local decoupling capacitor should be placed near each IC supply pin to reduce transient supply disturbance. The ground return should be continuous and should not force input signals to share long paths with noisy switching currents. During PCB layout, the three shift registers should be placed with attention to the connector input paths and the serial chain connections.The three 74HC165 devices are intended to capture the logic states associated with cable input contacts. Their serial chain allows the STM8 controller to read the combined input pattern without requiring a separate controller pin for every input.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1004 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 347 0 R
/E (A firmware mapping table should list each cable connector contact, its net label, the 74HC165 reference, the parallel input pin and the serial bit index. This mapping is necessary for detecting opens, shorts and crossed connections. The circuit's ability to distinguish each fault depends on the output stimulus and input network as well as the shift registers. During hardware bring-up, verify the supply voltage at each shift register and check that the load, clock and enable signals reach all devices. A logic analyser or oscilloscope can be used to observe the control signals and serial data output. A simple test pattern can be applied to the parallel inputs to verify bit order. For example, one input can be changed at a time while the serial data stream is observed. This should be performed with safe logic levels and without connecting an unverified cable fixture.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1005 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 347 0 R
/E (The 74HC165 provides a practical method for expanding digital input acquisition. Three cascaded devices provide 24 input positions and share common control signals. Correct operation depends on defined input levels, correct cascade wiring, timing compliance, bit mapping and suitable decoupling.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
