/E (During PCB implementation using KiCad, the assigned SOP-16 footprint must be checked against the manufacturer's mechanical package drawing to ensure that the physical dimensions and pad arrangement are correct. The four devices should be positioned according to their associated output connections so that the signal paths remain organized and unnecessary track crossings are minimized. Their placement should also consider the connections to the corresponding cable interfaces, power supply, and other supporting circuit components. Adequate spacing should be maintained between components to support proper soldering, inspection, and troubleshooting. The silkscreen layer should contain clearly visible component reference designators and pin identification markings wherever required. These considerations help improve the readability, assembly quality, and maintainability of the PCB. The footprint assignment must also be checked against the PCB manufacturer's fabrication requirements and the intended assembly process to avoid mechanical and connectivity issues.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1049 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 351 0 R
/E (The verification process for these devices begins with confirming the exact manufacturer and part number using the Bill of Materials \(BOM\), supplier records, or the marking available on the physical component. The corresponding datasheet and package drawing must then be obtained to establish the electrical and mechanical specifications. The datasheet pin configuration should be compared with the KiCad schematic symbol to verify that the electrical connections are correct, while the package dimensions should be compared with the assigned PCB footprint to confirm physical compatibility. The operating voltage, current limitations, output characteristics, power dissipation, and protection requirements must also be reviewed to determine whether the selected device is suitable for the intended cable testing application. If the datasheet cannot be obtained, the component should be treated as an unresolved design item and should not be approved for PCB fabrication until its identity, pin configuration, and electrical characteristics have been confirmed. A clear photograph of the component's top marking or a close-up view of the schematic symbol may help in identifying the correct part.)
/Pg 47 0 R
/K [2]
