endobj
1180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 361 0 R
/E (The keypad circuit is designed to provide user interaction with the STM8-based cable testing system. It consists of four individual switch sections that allow the user to provide input commands to the microcontroller. Each switch is connected through an associated resistor network to establish the required electrical conditions for reliable input detection. The switches are connected to separate signal lines, enabling the microcontroller to identify individual key operations.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 361 0 R
/E (The circuit uses appropriate pull-up or pull-down arrangements to maintain stable logic levels when the switches are not activated. When a switch is pressed, the corresponding signal state changes, allowing the microcontroller to detect the input. The resistors help maintain proper signal conditions and prevent undefined logic states. Each switch is connected to its respective input signal line, allowing individual key operations to be monitored by the controller.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 361 0 R
/E (The keypad interface enables the user to interact with the cable tester and initiate or control the required operations. The microcontroller reads the switch signals through its input pins and processes the corresponding commands according to the firmware. This arrangement provides a simple and convenient method of user input and supports the overall operation of the cable testing system. The complete keypad circuit schematic containing the four switch sections and their associated connections is shown in Fig. 4.4.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
