1428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 377 0 R
/E (A PCB footprint is the physical representation of an electronic component on a printed circuit board \(PCB\). It defines the positions, sizes and shapes of the copper pads, along with the component outline and placement information required for mounting and soldering. Each footprint is designed according to the physical dimensions and terminal arrangement of the corresponding electronic component. Selecting the correct footprint is important to ensure proper component placement, electrical connectivity and reliable assembly.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1429 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 377 0 R
/T (Main Elements of a PCB Footprint)
/E (Main Elements of a PCB Footprint)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
1430 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 377 0 R
/E (A PCB footprint consists of several important elements. Pads are the copper areas on which the component terminals are soldered, providing electrical and mechanical connections. The silkscreen layer displays component outlines, reference designators and markings that help identify components during assembly and inspection. The courtyard defines the recommended clearance area around a component and helps prevent overlapping placements. The fabrication layer represents the physical outline and dimensions of the component, providing useful information for manufacturing and assembly. The solder mask defines the regions of the PCB where the copper pads are exposed for soldering, while the paste mask defines the openings through which solder paste is applied during surface-mount assembly.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
1431 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 377 0 R
/T (Types of PCB Footprints)
/E (Types of PCB Footprints)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
1432 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 377 0 R
/E (PCB footprints are broadly classified into two main types: Through-Hole Technology \(THT\) and Surface-Mount Device \(SMD\) footprints. In Through-Hole Technology, component leads are inserted through drilled holes in the PCB and soldered to copper pads. This method provides strong mechanical connections and is commonly used for connectors, headers and certain switches. In Surface-Mount Technology, components are mounted directly onto copper pads on the PCB surface without requiring lead-insertion holes. SMD footprints support compact board designs, higher component density and automated assembly processes.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
1433 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 377 0 R
/T (Footprint Verification and PCB Updating)
/E (Footprint Verification and PCB Updating)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
1434 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 377 0 R
/E (After selecting a suitable footprint, it is important to verify its pad numbers, dimensions, spacing and physical arrangement against the component datasheet. The pad numbering in the footprint must correspond correctly to the pin numbering of the schematic symbol.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
1435 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 377 0 R
/E (The package dimensions, pin-one identification, pad size and spacing should also be checked to avoid errors during PCB assembly. Once the footprint assignments are completed and verified, the PCB layout is updated from the schematic using KiCad's Update PCB from Schematic feature. This transfers the assigned footprints into the PCB editor for further component placement and routing.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
